          "session_id": "full-g-md-009",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My TSH is a little high but my free T4 is still in the normal range. What does that mean, what does guidance describe about treating it, and how often should it be rechecked?",
          "answer": "A slightly elevated Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH) with a normal free T4 level is known as subclinical hypothyroidism [guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring, condition-hypothyroidism]. This means that your TSH is a little high, but your thyroid gland is still producing enough thyroid hormone (T4) [condition-hypothyroidism].\n\nGuidance differs on whether to treat subclinical hypothyroidism [Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, General clinical reference, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring]. There is broad agreement to treat when TSH is persistently above 10 mIU/L [Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, General clinical reference, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring]. For TSH levels between the upper normal limit and 10 mIU/L, some guidance suggests a trial of treatment if symptoms, positive thyroid antibodies, or cardiovascular risk factors are present [Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, General clinical reference, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring]. Other guidance recommends monitoring alone for most people in this range, noting that studies in older adults have shown no symptomatic benefit from treatment [Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, General clinical reference, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring]. Guidance also differs on whether the normal TSH range should be adjusted higher for older adults [guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring]. Pregnancy is a special situation with lower treatment thresholds and specific ranges for each trimester [Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, General clinical reference, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring].\n\nThe provided information states that if levothyroxine treatment is started, the dose is rechecked six to eight weeks after any change [condition-hypothyroidism]. However, the available information does not specify how often subclinical hypothyroidism should be rechecked if it is being monitored without treatment [condition-hypothyroidism, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring]. You should discuss with your clinician how often your thyroid function should be rechecked [condition-hypothyroidism].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
